seed thieves had a notoriously choppy  launch I'll be the first to admit it  I played for a few weeks back then and  quickly lost interest it was fun to set  sail with some friends but there just  wasn't enough to do after two years of  continuous work from the developers are  rare there's finally treasure here worth  digging for here's a scenario it's 3:00  a.m. your two-person sloop is anchored  at an outpost while you sell off the  loot from a 5-hour voyage before you  start did you raise the gold hoarders  emissary flag and throughout the night  you work to rank it up to level five now  every chest trinket and gem you sell is  worth two and a half times its normal  value but there's a catch sailing with  an emissary flag puts a giant red X on  your back another crew sinks your ship  and steals your flag they can sell it  for a high price and pirates who raise  the new Reapers bones faction flag can  even see you on their ships map if they  reach level 5 themselves in an instant  the worst thing imaginable happens and  your triumphant turnin becomes a fight  for your life another players sleep  rounds the corner behind your boat  adrenalin washes through your body like  an icy tidal wave but you saw them  coming way too late an enemy player has  already boarded your ship with an  explosive stronghold keg they set it off  as you hurl yourself from the deck  there's almost no way to recover from  this your entire boat is on fire the  hull is punched through with holes your  mast is down your steering wheel is  broken the capstan that raises your  anchor is half broken and the Cannonball  is being shot from the other boat are  knocking you around so badly that it's  hard to think  but somehow you're still alive you and  your crew mate repair the mast raise the  anchor put out the fires and lower the  sails all while bailing water  hammering planks over the gouges in the  hull and using your trusty blunderbuss  to fend off the other crews continued  attempts to board by this time your  hands are violently shaking you sail off  and do the only thing you can with the  pursuing ship directly behind you set  your boat on a clear heading jump off  the back and grab their ladders kill  them both and while they're waiting to  respawn you steer their boat onto some  rocks destroy their mast lower their  anchor and throw enough fire bombs to  light the whole thing up you catch a  mermaid back to your boat you did it you  lost them the hall is yours and once the  adrenaline dissipates from your system  you're going to sleep silently granted  hours later they need intended and with  vivid dreams of burning ships on a  colorful sea but still those kinds of  interactions with other players have  been seeing thieves main draw since the  game launched in 2018 but so many of the  particulars of that tense encounter I  just described were only possible  because of the countless features tools  and systems of the developers at rare  have added the game in the two years  since its release most recently in the  massive April 2020 update called ships  of fortune the fire that can spread  across your boat is new as are the  ability to revive fallen teammates the  damage that can be caused to masts the  harpoons adorning every ship the  rowboats you can find around the world  adorable pets that cost real money but  are totally worth it a new area of the  map fishing and cooking systems throw  bow grenades new world encounters like  skeleton fleets tough challenges like  the fort of the Damned and other small  and large surprises waiting to be  discovered on every new voyage  [Music]  but it's the emissary flags that have  really propelled save these into new  waters by incentivizing players in  various subtle ways the emissary system  solved many of the problems that have  plagued this game for years the risk  reward equation is simple the longer you  sail as an emissary the higher your  multiplier when you finally sell you  prizes but the more attractive you are  is a target for other players looking to  secure a payout without doing the work  themselves it alleviates some of the  game's intense grind by making it easier  to earn money in rank of factions  speeding up the climb to coveted pirate  legend status it encourages players to  seek one another out rather than avoid  conflict the long flapping emissary  flags are highly visible and a  high-level emissary spied in the  distance is a tempting prize to chase on  the other hand the emissary system is  completely optional if you don't raise  an emissary flag you'll get a lower  payout when you sell treasure at the end  of your voyage but you're also more  likely to be left alone by other players  see if thieves always have the  foundation of something great even from  its bare-bones launch it was a blast to  set sail with friends there just wasn't  much else to do but rares gradually fix  that with consistent content drops that  have added everything from story  missions to a PvP arena mode if you  haven't played in a while you're going  to discover a ton of new things the next  time you set sail granted as the game's  scope has continuously increased rare  has clearly found it challenging to keep  up with balance issues and bugs that  have remained present for a long time  that journey I described at the  beginning only stretched out to five  grueling hours long because a server  merge triggered a bug as they usually do  it caused us to lose control of our ship  ultimately sinking it that reset our  emissary rank and we had to spend hours  climbing back up to rank 5 to get the  best payout for the loot that we'd  already worked to earn fact that we  managed to get away is the only thing  separating that story of triumph from an  infuriating nightmare that probably  would have made me take a break from the  game for a few days there are plenty of  balance problems as well like the fact  that the blunderbusses damage feels like  it has more to do with your network pain  in your aim and nothing is more  frustrating than losing hours worth of  work not because you were surprised or  outmaneuvered by another player or due  to any mistake on your part but because  the game decided to spawn a Kraken a  Megalodon and a skeleton ship  simultaneously your location sometimes  while you're also in a storm or with a  dead  volcano erupting nearby the more time  you invest in sea of thieves the more  those instances seem to stack up  creating a laundry list in your mind of  all the things rare has failed to  address our improve and so much of this  game is designed to keep you playing the  grind to hip higher legend and unlock  the coolest stuff is way too intense for  most players one problem that emerges  the higher levels is the fact that short  sessions become increasingly unrewarding  creating a need to play for long  stretches without breaks literally if  you don't have around four hours to  commit at a certain point it's barely  worth embarking at all to compound that  the game regularly goes offline for  maintenance and patch releases but the  only in-game notification about it  appears just 15 minutes before the  server's shutdown which is barely long  enough to race to the nearest outpost  interior stuffing if you miss the  official tweet or reddit post about it  you're out of luck and you're likely to  feel like you wasted a bunch of time  climbing emissary ranks or fighting a  lengthy sea battle when the shutdown  arrives unexpectedly several hours into  your session on the other hand no matter  how high you climb you'll never actually  unlock any goner ship or tool that's  technically better than what everybody  else already has every single unlockable  in the game remains purely cosmetic to  this day what's the harm of some true  stretch goals for the most dedicated  pirates many players will never reach  those heights but unlike when see if  thieves launched at this point there's  no shortage of other things to do  besides chase cosmetics there's one  other thing I feel I need to address  there's a subset of players who to this  day think the game needs PvE servers  where you can't be attacked by other  players but the danger of being dumped  stirred by any other crew at any other  time is this games beating heart no  matter how good you think you are the  possibility of crushing defeat is always  lurking just behind the next wave it  just wouldn't be sea of thieves without  that since the game's launch over two  years ago rare has worked continuously  to build on see if thieves strong  framework and the countless features and  systems they've added have all enhanced  that core undeniable truth they still  have work to do and for some players  that will never be enough but there are  open seas and clear skies ahead for the  current and future pirate legends out  there and even for the players who will  never reach that level but simply want  to make some of their own tall tales out  on the unforgiving waves see if these is  finally a voyage worth embarking on  [Music]  [Applause]
 

